g the Erythraean coast"},{"sourceSlug":"http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/9d5b3a2e7f1c4a9bb8a2ac5aa8abdd9","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea","targetName":"Periplus of the Erythraean Sea","context":"The Periplus provides valuable insights into the economic and cultural dynamics of the region during the Roman Empire's peak"},{"sourceSlug":"http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/7f8d1e2b0c9a4b6af35ac5aa95ceadcf","sourceName":"Mocha (modern-day Yemen)","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea","targetName":"Periplus of the Erythraean Sea","context":"Historical significance as a major hub for trade between East and West"},{"sourceSlug":"http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/2d7c3df4c9f2b8e6ba1ac5a95c3ae9bf","sourceName":"Maritime trade networks","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea","targetName":"Periplus of the Erythraean Sea","context":"Detailed cartography and descriptions of ports and settlements"},{"sourceSlug":"http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/3c9d0e7f4f1c8b0a6ba1ac5a95c3ae9bf","sourceName":"Herodotus' Histories","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea","targetName":"Periplus of the Erythraean Sea","context":"Early knowledge of trade networks and commerce"}],"places":[{"name":"Mocha (modern-day Yemen)","role":"Major hub for trade between East and West"},{"name":"Alexandria, Egypt","role":"Port city and center of Greek culture"},{"name":"Persepolis, Persia","role":"Cultural and economic center of the Achaemenid Empire"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Periplus of the Erythraean Sea","type":"Reference article","slug":"periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q664162","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Periplus_of_the_Erythraean_Sea"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Periplus of the Erythraean Sea contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea (Greek: Περίπλους τῆς Ἐρυθρᾶς Θαλάσσης, Periplus Maris Erythraei) is an ancient Greek merchant's guide to the sea routes and trading ports of the Red Sea, the Arabian Peninsula, East Africa, the Persian Gulf, India, and Ceylon (Sri Lanka), written in the 1st century CE (most scholars date it c. 50–70 CE, though dates from 40–150 CE have been proposed) by an anonymous Greek-speaking Egyptian merchant based in Alexandria or the Egyptian Red Sea port of Myos Hormos or Berenice. It is a practical commercial document — a periplus (sailing guide around a sea or coast) — but is the single most important textual source for the commercial and geographic world of the Indian Ocean trade in the Roman period.

The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea describes, in order from the Egyptian Red Sea ports northward and then southward to Africa (Adulis, Malao, Opone, Rhapta) and eastward through the Arabian Peninsula (Arabia Eudaemon, Muza, Kanê) to India (Barygaza, Muziris, Nelcynda, Poduca, and beyond to the Ganges and the Chryse region), the ports and anchorages, the goods available for trade and the goods required, the political authorities, the navigation conditions, and the prevailing winds. The document's commercial intelligence is precise and detailed — it distinguishes between the goods imported by different ports (wine, olive oil, copper, tin, lead, coral, glass for India; ivory, rhinoceros horn, tortoise shell, incense, silk, pepper, cotton, indigo, and nard in exchange) and provides practical advice on the best season for trading voyages.

The Periplus is the primary textual evidence for the extraordinary scale and integration of the Roman-period Indian Ocean trade — connecting the Roman Empire to Arabia, East Africa, India, and possibly China through a network of maritime commerce that moved luxury goods, bulk commodities, and precious metals across the ancient world. Archaeological discoveries at Indian sites (Arikamedu, Pattanam) of Roman goods (coins, amphorae, pottery) confirm and extend the Periplus's picture.","subjects":["Greek Literature","Ancient Era","Roman Empire","Trade History","Indian Ocean","Maritime History","Geography","Commercial History"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea provides invaluable insights into ancient maritime commerce, trade networks, and cultural exchange between East and West. Its detailed cartography and descriptions of ports and settlements have shaped our understanding of the region's economic and cultural dynamics during the Roman Empire's peak.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
